## leaf wait --help
Usage: leaf wait [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Watch every page this session holds, or observe PAGE without taking ownership.
  Claim a foreign page explicitly with `leaf page claim`.

  Print one page's complete ordered batch, thread context, and response
  requirements as an immutable delivery, whose `acknowledge` says how to confirm
  it. `leaf delivery read <id>` reads that same delivery. Where the harness's
  hook carries input into the turn, as in Claude Code, print one line naming the
  page with new input instead, and end.

Options:
  --ack DELIVERY_ID  Confirm receipt of this complete delivery before waiting.
  --help             Show this message and exit.

## leaf delivery read --help
Usage: leaf delivery read [OPTIONS] DELIVERY_ID

  Print DELIVERY_ID, or one part of a large one, without confirming it.

  Each part except the last gives the command for the next part in `next`; the
  last gives in `acknowledge` how to confirm the delivery.

Options:
  --part INTEGER RANGE  Read this numbered part of a large delivery.  [x>=1]
  --help                Show this message and exit.

## leaf delivery ack --help
Usage: leaf delivery ack [OPTIONS] DELIVERY_ID

  Confirm that every part of DELIVERY_ID is in your context, so its updates read
  Picked up. Reread any part whose output is missing first.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page state --help
Usage: leaf page state [OPTIONS] PAGE [ID]

  Print the page as it is now, as one JSON object: the active revision's file,
  the current widget actions and workers' reports, the Questions and Tasks
  lists, thread summaries, versions, presence, and bound data. Read the active
  revision's HTML beside it for the document.

  ID narrows the output to what it names. A message, or a widget frozen into
  one, names its thread: the output is that thread with a page of its messages,
  their frozen markup, and the state of its widgets, paged by --after and
  --limit. A widget on the page names itself: its element, its current actions
  and reports, its asks and to-dos, and its workflows.

Options:
  --after SEQ            a thread's history after this sequence (default: from
                         its start)  [x>=0]
  --limit INTEGER RANGE  how many of a thread's messages to print (default: 50)
                         [1<=x<=100]
  --help                 Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread summarize --help
Usage: leaf thread summarize [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Replace --from through --through in the panel with a Markdown summary.

  Both name messages in one thread, which is the thread summarized. The original
  messages remain in the append-only transcript and can always be revealed. A
  later overlapping summary replaces this one.

Options:
  --from MESSAGE     [required]
  --through MESSAGE  [required]
  --text TEXT        summary Markdown; pass '' to fold without prose (default:
                     stdin)
  --label TEXT       disclosure label (default: Earlier discussion)
  --help             Show this message and exit.

## leaf status --help
Usage: leaf status [OPTIONS] PAGE {waiting|idle} [DETAIL]

  Say the page waits on its user, or that you are done with it.

  Use waiting with DETAIL naming the answer you want from the user; waiting
  without DETAIL invites text comments. Use idle when you are finished; it is
  refused while the page has updates, workers' reports or page errors nobody has
  picked up, updates you owe an answer, or to-dos of yours still open. Either
  one clears every `leaf task start` you made, so no update or to-do reads
  Working; your to-dos stay open. Status has no working state: to show what you
  are working on, run `leaf task start`.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread open --help
Usage: leaf thread open [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Open a thread as the agent (--text or stdin): anchored where --quote or
  --section points at a passage, general where neither does \u2014 a question about
  the work as a whole.

  The user answers it in the browser. Refuses a quote the active revision does
  not hold, or holds more than once. The comment's id is the thread's.

Options:
  --quote TEXT   passage text from the active revision
  --section ID   element ID to anchor or scope --quote
  --part ID      declared visual part within --section
  --text TEXT    comment text (default: stdin)
  --markup TEXT  HTML fragment after the text, validated here
  --title TEXT   name the thread for the panel unless it has a name: a few
                 words, at most 80 characters
  --help         Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread reply --help
Usage: leaf thread reply [OPTIONS] PAGE THREAD

  Post a new agent message to THREAD (--text or stdin).

  A message is refused while the thread owes a response; answer it with `leaf
  response reply <answer.ref>`. --ephemeral posts progress. Saved page edits are
  validated and activated before posting.

Options:
  --title TEXT   name the thread for the panel unless it has a name: a few
                 words, at most 80 characters
  --ephemeral    one-line progress, without answering
  --todo TITLE   the reply promises work it doesn't deliver; opens that to-do on
                 the thread, ended by `leaf task end` with the reply's id
  --ask          the reply asks the user something, which waits on them as an
                 ask
  --markup TEXT  frozen HTML fragment after the text
  --text TEXT    reply text (default: stdin)
  --detach       remove the thread's page target
  --part ID      new declared visual part within --section
  --section ID   new element ID, or scope for --quote
  --quote TEXT   new passage text to move this thread onto
  --help         Show this message and exit.

## leaf response reply --help
Usage: leaf response reply [OPTIONS] REFERENCE

  Reply to the update REFERENCE names: the `answer.ref` its delivery printed.

  An answer says what it leaves the thread with: --done when it delivers what
  the message asked for, --ask when it asks the user something back, or --todo
  TITLE when it promises work it doesn't deliver, which stays on you as a to-do.
  Progress (--ephemeral) and failure receipts (--failure) take none of them.

  The same command sets text, frozen HTML, the thread's title and a new anchor.
  It validates and activates saved page edits, then posts the reply at once.
  Once an answer posts, your turn's final message is not posted as a second
  reply; progress (--ephemeral) does not stop that. Rerunning a reply that
  posted prints the earlier reply instead of posting again; give a retried
  --ephemeral progress line an --attempt so it posts once.

Options:
  --attempt TEXT  retry key: a rerun with the same key posts nothing new
                  (default: the reference, except with --ephemeral)
  --failure TEXT  failure code when no answer is coming; refused while your
                  turn's final message is to be the reply
  --done          the reply delivers what the message asked for
  --title TEXT    name the thread for the panel unless it has a name: a few
                  words, at most 80 characters
  --ephemeral     one-line progress, without answering
  --todo TITLE    the reply promises work it doesn't deliver; opens that to-do
                  on the thread, ended by `leaf task end` with the reply's id
  --ask           the reply asks the user something, which waits on them as an
                  ask
  --markup TEXT   frozen HTML fragment after the text
  --text TEXT     reply text (default: stdin)
  --detach        remove the thread's page target
  --part ID       new declared visual part within --section
  --section ID    new element ID, or scope for --quote
  --quote TEXT    new passage text to move this thread onto
  --help          Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread edit --help
Usage: leaf thread edit [OPTIONS] PAGE MESSAGE

  Replace the visible text of MESSAGE, an agent-authored comment or reply, or
  with --title rename the thread MESSAGE is in, whoever opened it.

  The original and every revision remain in the append-only event log. Frozen
  markup is not editable. Keep a title stable unless the thread's subject
  changes.

Options:
  --text TEXT   replacement text (default: stdin, unless --title)
  --title TEXT  rename MESSAGE's thread for the panel: a few words, at most 80
                characters
  --help        Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread resolve --help
Usage: leaf thread resolve [OPTIONS] PAGE THREAD

  Close THREAD, by any message in it, as the agent.

  The user ordinarily closes a thread; the Leaf skill's references/threads.md
  says when the agent does. Refuses a thread that asked for a version until a
  stamped version answers it. The panel names who resolved it.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page report --help
Usage: leaf page report [OPTIONS] PAGE WIDGET VERB [NAME=VALUE]...

  Report a state change onto a page widget, as a worker.

  The verb and its fields are the widget's own agent-written x-state verb \u2014
  `leaf page report <page> t-parser status value=review text="Ready for review"`
  moves a task. The page paints the report live as provisional news; it stands
  until a version absorbs or overrules it, and the page's watcher wakes to fold
  it in.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page check --help
Usage: leaf page check [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Check PAGE/index.html.

  Runs deterministic markup checks. --render also checks the drawn page in the
  host's browser: whichever executable LEAF_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE, CHROME_PATH, or
  CHROME_BIN names, else the installed Chrome, else the first browser on PATH. A
  host with no browser gets a note in place of each browser check.

Options:
  --render  also check the rendered page in the host's browser
  --help    Show this message and exit.

## leaf page stamp --help
Usage: leaf page stamp [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Stamp PAGE/index.html with a changelog.

  Checks the exact source first, then records it as the next public version.
  Repeat --completes for each widget whose open to-dos this version completes,
  which ends them done, citing the version. A to-do otherwise remains open,
  including when a revision removes its widget. --from-directory replaces
  index.html and the complete page/ tree atomically with stamping; --if-source
  is the authored-input digest read through leaf.publishing, refusing a
  competing edit.

Options:
  --text TEXT                 changelog text (default: stdin)
  --from-directory DIRECTORY  replace index.html and page/ together from this
                              candidate directory
  --if-source TEXT            expected authored-input digest, required with
                              --from-directory
  --completes WIDGET          a widget whose open to-dos this version completes
                              (repeatable)
  --help                      Show this message and exit.
